  "prediction": "**Early Withdrawals of Retirement Savings: Incidence, Factors, and Strategies**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report examining the incidence and factors contributing to early withdrawals of retirement savings from 401(k) plans and Individual Retirement Accounts (IRAs). The report highlights the importance of understanding these withdrawals, which can have significant long-term impacts on individuals' retirement security.\n\n**Incidence of Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report estimates that in 2013, individuals ages 25 to 55 withdrew approximately $68.7 billion from their retirement accounts, with IRA owners accounting for the largest share ($39.5 billion). The majority of these withdrawals were due to hardship, cashouts, or loan defaults. The report also notes that the incidence of early withdrawals is higher among certain demographic groups, including those with lower incomes, education levels, and household debt.\n\n**Factors Contributing to Early Withdrawals**\n\nStakeholders interviewed for the report identified several factors contributing to early withdrawals, including:\n\n1. Financial shocks, such as job loss or medical expenses\n2. Limited financial literacy and planning\n3. Plan rules and policies, such as loan repayment terms and hardship withdrawal criteria\n4. Employer practices, such as cashing out balances at job separation\n\n**Strategies to Reduce Early Withdrawals**\n\nThe report identifies several strategies that may reduce the incidence and amount of early withdrawals, including:\n\n1. Simplifying the rollover process\n2. Restricting cashouts to participant contributions only\n3. Narrowing the IRS safe harbor for hardship withdrawals\n4. Replacing hardship withdrawals with hardship loans\n5. Incorporating emergency savings features into 401(k) plans\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe GAO recommends that the Secretary of Labor direct the Assistant Secretary for EBSA to revise the Form 5500 to require plan sponsors to report qualified plan loan offsets as a separate line item distinct from other types of distributions.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe report highlights the importance of understanding the factors contributing to early withdrawals of retirement savings and the need for strategies to reduce these withdrawals. By implementing these strategies, employers, policymakers, and individuals can work together to promote retirement security and reduce the long-term impacts of early withdrawals.",
